1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,643
This video shows you how to corner faster and more safely in short track.

2
00:00:04,643 --> 00:00:13,165
You will learn the four things that make a good corner, the mistakes that cost you speed, and what to check when your blades slip out.

3
00:00:13,165 --> 00:00:19,717
Short track is skated anticlockwise on a tight oval, so almost half of every lap is spent turning left.

4
00:00:19,717 --> 00:00:22,897
That makes the corner the core skill of the sport.

5
00:00:22,897 --> 00:00:25,123
It is also where races are decided.

6
00:00:25,123 --> 00:00:32,056
The skaters who carry speed through the bend and come out ready to attack are the ones who move to the front.

7
00:00:32,056 --> 00:00:37,462
This guide is for skaters who can already skate the oval and want more from the bend.

8
00:00:38,162 --> 00:00:42,141
Good cornering comes down to four things, done in this order.

9
00:00:42,141 --> 00:00:45,924
First, drop into a low position before you reach the bend.

10
00:00:45,924 --> 00:00:50,881
Second, lean your whole body and both blades towards the centre of the turn.

11
00:00:50,881 --> 00:00:55,055
Third, keep both legs pushing with crossovers all the way round.

12
00:00:55,055 --> 00:01:00,273
Fourth, let the inside hand brush the ice as a balance reference, not a support.

13
00:01:00,273 --> 00:01:05,491
Get those four right and the corner stops being the part of the lap you survive.

14
00:01:05,491 --> 00:01:07,774
It becomes the part where you gain.

15
00:01:08,474 --> 00:01:10,617
It all starts with body position.

16
00:01:10,617 --> 00:01:15,032
Bend deeply at the knees and hips so your hips sit low over the ice.

17
00:01:15,032 --> 00:01:17,499
Keep your chest calm and your head up.

18
00:01:17,499 --> 00:01:21,329
Look through the exit of the corner, not down at your feet.

19
00:01:21,329 --> 00:01:23,342
This front view shows the lean.

20
00:01:23,342 --> 00:01:31,652
Your whole body tips towards the centre of the turn, so your body and skates tip left together, like a motorcycle taking a bend.

21
00:01:31,652 --> 00:01:34,834
Your weight drives down through the inside edges.

22
00:01:34,834 --> 00:01:37,301
The lean is what lets your edges bite.

23
00:01:37,301 --> 00:01:41,456
If you stay upright, your blades cannot grip and you slide wide.

24
00:01:41,456 --> 00:01:44,962
So commit to the lean and trust the edges to hold you.

25
00:01:45,662 --> 00:01:48,665
You do not coast through a short track corner.

26
00:01:48,665 --> 00:01:51,080
You power through it with crossovers.

27
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:53,626
Here we are looking down on the corner.

28
00:01:53,626 --> 00:02:00,220
On a left turn, your right foot lifts, travels over the left skate, and sets down on its inside edge.

29
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:04,006
As it lands, it takes your weight and starts its own push.

30
00:02:04,006 --> 00:02:12,362
At the same time, your left leg pushes out and slightly back from underneath, then recovers under your body for the next stroke.

31
00:02:12,362 --> 00:02:15,626
The left push is a real power stroke, not a glide.

32
00:02:15,626 --> 00:02:19,543
Press the whole blade into the ice and extend the leg fully.

33
00:02:19,543 --> 00:02:20,914
Timing is everything.

34
00:02:20,914 --> 00:02:23,329
Cross too early and your feet tangle.

35
00:02:23,329 --> 00:02:25,548
Cross too late and you lose drive.

36
00:02:25,548 --> 00:02:28,356
Practise slowly at first, then build speed.

37
00:02:29,055 --> 00:02:35,153
Through the deepest part of the corner, most skaters place the left hand lightly on the ice.

38
00:02:35,153 --> 00:02:37,207
It is not there to hold you up.

39
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:42,840
It is a light touch that tells you where the ice is and helps you commit to the lean.

40
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:48,142
You are brushing the ice, not leaning on it, because pressing down scrubs speed.

41
00:02:48,142 --> 00:02:49,402
Timing matters too.

42
00:02:49,402 --> 00:02:56,360
As the diagram shows, the hand comes down as you settle into the apex and lifts as you rise for the exit.

43
00:02:56,360 --> 00:03:00,734
Do not reach early on entry, and do not drag it onto the straight.

44
00:03:00,734 --> 00:03:03,452
Keep it just behind and inside your body.

45
00:03:03,452 --> 00:03:10,675
Low friction glove tips help the hand slide, and cut resistant gloves protect you near other skaters' blades.

46
00:03:11,375 --> 00:03:14,128
Most corners are lost before you reach them.

47
00:03:14,128 --> 00:03:22,385
If you arrive tall, drifting and still deciding where to go, the first third of the turn gets spent on corrections instead of speed.

48
00:03:22,385 --> 00:03:26,263
Use the last two strides on the straight to finish three jobs.

49
00:03:26,263 --> 00:03:27,139
Set your line.

50
00:03:27,139 --> 00:03:32,143
Finish getting low, so your hips are at corner height and your knees are loaded.

51
00:03:32,143 --> 00:03:37,335
And turn your head, so your eyes are through the bend before your skates get there.

52
00:03:37,335 --> 00:03:39,899
Your shoulders and hips follow your eyes.

53
00:03:39,899 --> 00:03:41,338
Here is a simple check.

54
00:03:41,338 --> 00:03:45,279
If your first crossover feels rushed or cramped, you were late.

55
00:03:45,279 --> 00:03:50,658
Start your lean one stride earlier for a few laps and see if the corner feels roomier.

56
00:03:51,358 --> 00:03:53,697
Think of the corner in three parts.

57
00:03:53,697 --> 00:03:58,575
On entry, you arrive already low and already leaning, with your line set.

58
00:03:58,575 --> 00:04:04,321
At the apex, the tightest point of the turn beside the block, you are at maximum lean.

59
00:04:04,321 --> 00:04:08,598
Your crossovers are flowing and your inside hand grazes the ice.

60
00:04:08,598 --> 00:04:16,951
On exit, you rise slightly, direct your energy forward, and accelerate onto the straight with your eyes already up the track.

61
00:04:16,951 --> 00:04:22,497
The skater who gets all three parts right carries the most speed onto the straight.

62
00:04:23,197 --> 00:04:28,554
A blade that washes out mid corner is the most common complaint in short track.

63
00:04:28,554 --> 00:04:32,826
There are four causes, and you should check them in this order.

64
00:04:32,826 --> 00:04:33,979
First, your body.

65
00:04:33,979 --> 00:04:42,117
Being too upright is by far the most common cause for a developing skater, because the edge has almost no angle to bite.

66
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:45,236
Film one corner from the side and from behind.

67
00:04:45,236 --> 00:04:46,525
Second, your edges.

68
00:04:46,525 --> 00:04:50,458
Draw a fingernail lightly across the edge, never along it.

69
00:04:50,458 --> 00:04:52,492
A crisp edge catches the nail.

70
00:04:52,492 --> 00:04:56,561
Third, your blade setup, meaning bend, rocker and alignment.

71
00:04:56,561 --> 00:04:58,392
Change one thing at a time.

72
00:04:58,392 --> 00:04:59,477
Fourth, the ice.

73
00:04:59,477 --> 00:05:06,055
Soft, late session ice gives less grip, so carry a little less speed and apply pressure smoothly.

74
00:05:06,055 --> 00:05:10,123
A setup change made too early hides what your body is doing.

75
00:05:10,823 --> 00:05:14,015
Most lost time comes from a short list of habits.

76
00:05:14,015 --> 00:05:15,513
Fix them one at a time.

77
00:05:15,513 --> 00:05:22,416
Standing too tall shrinks your lean, so hold a low position for one full corner, even if you skate slower.

78
00:05:22,416 --> 00:05:28,343
Looking down pulls your shoulders in, so pick a point at the exit and hold your eyes there.

79
00:05:28,343 --> 00:05:36,614
Leaning with the upper body only gives you the look of a lean without the edge angle, so tip from the ankles and hips together.

80
00:05:36,614 --> 00:05:44,169
Pressing hard on the inside hand takes weight off the blades, so practise with the hand hovering just above the ice.

81
00:05:44,169 --> 00:05:50,552
Rising early on the exit throws away speed, so stay low until your skates point down the straight.

82
00:05:50,552 --> 00:05:55,762
And if you cross over too early, slow the pattern down until each push finishes.

83
00:05:56,462 --> 00:05:59,020
Corners improve with focused practice.

84
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:02,250
Give each drill a few sessions before moving on.

85
00:06:02,250 --> 00:06:06,827
Start with slow laps, thinking only about a clean crossover pattern.

86
00:06:06,827 --> 00:06:11,605
Then do low holds, staying in your deepest position from entry to exit.

87
00:06:11,605 --> 00:06:18,941
Next, trace the corner with your inside hand just off the ice, then add gentle contact through the apex only.

88
00:06:18,941 --> 00:06:26,950
After that, drive the first four strides onto the straight as hard as you can, and watch whether you stand up to do it.

89
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:30,449
Finally, film yourself from the side and from above.

90
00:06:30,449 --> 00:06:38,862
If your ankle is sharply painful, swollen or numb rather than simply tired, stop skating and see a physiotherapist or doctor.

91
00:06:39,562 --> 00:06:40,485
So, to sum up.

92
00:06:40,485 --> 00:06:43,253
Get low and set your line before the bend.

93
00:06:43,253 --> 00:06:46,087
Lean your whole body so the edges can bite.

94
00:06:46,087 --> 00:06:48,987
Push with both legs through every crossover.

95
00:06:48,987 --> 00:06:53,139
And let the inside hand brush the ice, never carry your weight.

96
00:06:53,139 --> 00:06:59,269
When your blades slip, check your body first, then your edges, then your setup, then the ice.

97
00:06:59,269 --> 00:07:08,100
The full guide on the LiveLica blog covers how your boots, blades and gloves support your cornering, plus answers to common questions.

98
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,143
The link is in the description.
